  "meta": {
    "title": "Sipser’s M₂: strings of 0s whose length is a power of 2",
    "blurb": "Decides {0^(2ⁿ) | n ≥ 0} by halving: each sweep crosses off every other 0, and rejects if a sweep ever finds an odd number greater than one. The state names are Sipser’s, from Example 3.7.",
    "inputs": [
      {
        "w": "0",
        "expect": "accept",
        "label": "2⁰"
      },
      {
        "w": "00",
        "expect": "accept",
        "label": "2¹"
      },
      {
        "w": "0000",
        "expect": "accept",
        "label": "2²"
      },
      {
        "w": "00000000",
        "expect": "accept",
        "label": "2³"
      },
      {
        "w": "",
        "expect": "reject",
        "label": "0 is not a power of 2"
      },
      {
        "w": "000",
        "expect": "reject",
        "label": "3"
      },
      {
        "w": "000000",
        "expect": "reject",
        "label": "6"
      },
      {
        "w": "0000000",
        "expect": "reject",
        "label": "7"
      }
    ],
    "library": {
      "author": {
        "login": "thethinkmachine"
      },
      "license": "CC-BY-4.0",
      "tags": [
        "sipser",
        "decider",
        "textbook",
        "not-context-free"
      ],
      "difficulty": "intro",
      "chapter": "Sipser, Introduction to the Theory of Computation, Example 3.7",
      "readme": "Sipser's own description of M₂:\n\n1. Sweep left to right across the tape, crossing off every other 0.\n2. If in stage 1 the tape contained a single 0, *accept*.\n3. If in stage 1 the tape contained more than a single 0 and the number of 0s was odd, *reject*.\n4. Return the head to the left-hand end of the tape.\n5. Go to stage 1.\n\n**q3** and **q4** are the parity of the 0s seen in the current sweep; **q5** walks back. The very first 0 is overwritten with a blank rather than an x, which is how the machine finds the left end of the tape again. The rejecting state is left out: in this app a machine with no move rejects.\n\nCompare **Powers of two, by halving**, the same idea on a linear bounded automaton."
    }
  }
}
